<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3601"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nadeltanz<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nadeltanz<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 3:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Anyone have any recommendations on good databases for research. I am familiar with LEXIS/NEXIS far as knowing it&#39;s a household name. I think I read that Prentice-Hall had one. I read a message that Tom said he thought B. was a faculty member at WSU <BR>way back when. This way I could check the faculty <BR>members probably during the time period B. was active. If B. could be the culprit in the victimizing of Carol Mould, we have a better chance of catching him as opposed to the cold cases.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
